TY  - JOUR
T1  - Chapter 7 - Physiology: Energy Acquisition and Utilisation
AN  - 1808673495; PQ0003398879
AB  - This review summarises current understanding of what scallops are feeding upon and how they acquire energy through feeding activity and expend energy through their metabolic processes. While the principles of feeding activity are consistent across many species, there are major differences in the efficiency and retention of food particles related to variations in the characteristics of the gill type. Differences in life histories and the wide variety of habitats exploited by scallops are reflected in substantial differences in the rate of clearance of particles, particularly as it relates to the concentration of food. Many of the differences observed in feeding activity within and between scallop species over the years and the lack of consistent trends and patterns of feeding behaviour could be attributed to differences in techniques used. One major factor could be the reliance in the earlier studies on cultured microalgae as a homogeneous food supply and the focus in more recent studies on natural assemblages of particles as experimental food sources. We have come to recognise that scallops behave differently than other species of bivalves such as mussels even when they are studied simultaneously using the identical food supply. Like other species of bivalves, scallops produce pseudofeces as a mechanism to alter food composition before ingestion and the efficiency of this selection process has been shown to vary between species and environmental conditions. There have been no consistent relationships between absorption efficiency and any characteristics of the food supply in the scallop species studied to date. Current velocity can have a neutral or positive influence on feeding and growth at low or moderate velocities, but both feeding and growth are reduced at the higher velocities tested. Harmful and toxic algae produce a variety of negative effects on scallops dependent upon the algal species and their concentration, the pectinid species and its life stage, just to mention a few. Reductions in clearance rate have been cited for some species and will have a significant impact on the acquisition of energy to support maintenance, growth and reproduction. The measurement of oxygen consumption as an indicator of metabolic energy expenditure in scallops is known to vary with body size, physiological condition and most environmental conditions assessed. The availability of ambient oxygen, seasonal activity related to reproductive condition and/or temperature and activity level associated with swimming are all known to influence oxygen consumption and have significant impacts on energy expenditure (see Chapter 12 by Guderley and Tremblay for more detail on the physiological and biochemical responses of scallop swimming behaviour). Nitrogenous excretion may represent a significant loss of energy for some pectinids under certain stressful conditions however byssus production is thought to only represent a minor component of the energy budget. The amount of energy available to support growth varies with size and age as the investment in energy for reproduction usually increases with age in the longer lived species of pectinids. Pectinids exhibit a wide range of growth rates related to differences in life histories and longevity often associated with distribution patterns, latitude and water depth that influence their environmental conditions such as temperature and food supply.
