TY  - JOUR
T1  - Carbon isotopic records of middle Holocene corals and environmental causes in northern South China Sea
AN  - 1705067725; 21067739
AB  - Two high-resolution delta super(13)C records of similar to 5.4 and similar to 4.4 ka coral colonies Porites lutea from east coast of Hainan Island in the northern South China Sea (SCS) provide insights into the complex links between coral carbon isotopes and environment changes. The delta super(13)C of the similar to 5.4 ka coral offered a 40-year growth history with an average of -2.62ppt and amplitude of 2.96ppt, the similar to 4.4 ka coral's revealed a 55-year growth history with an average of -3.12ppt and amplitude of 4.21ppt, which revealed higher coral delta super(13)C and increased seasonality in the middle Holocene. Our analysis suggests that the higher coral delta super(13)C was the combined result of higher radiation, salinity and nutrient level and lower atmosphere CO sub(2) concentration, which might increase coral delta super(13)C through strengthening photosynthesis of symbiont zooxanthella in the context of kinetic and metabolic fractionations. The increased seasonality was mostly produced by strengthened radiation. The decreasing standard deviation of the similar to 5.4 ka coral annual delta super(13)C revealed weakened interannual changes of atmospheric CO sub(2), salinity, and nutrient. By contrast, the increasing deviation of the similar to 4.4 ka coral delta super(13)C suggests strengthened interannual changes of these variables. The similar to 5.4 ka coral delta super(13)C showed a long-term increasing trend at a rate of 0.33% increase y super(-1), which was attributed to natural decrease in atmospheric CO sub(2), and increasing salinity and nutrient level. However, the similar to 4.4 ka coral delta super(13)C showed a long term decreasing trend at a rate of 0.25% decrease y super(-1), which was ascribed to unusual increase in atmospheric CO sub(2), and decreasing salinity and nutrient level.
