TY  - JOUR
T1  - Loss of zooxanthellae in a coral under high seawater temperature and nutrient enrichment
AN  - 1627968910; 20928065
AB  - To investigate the effect of nutrient concentrations on coral-algal symbiosis under thermal stress, the abundance and release of zooxanthellae in the coral Acropora tenuis were quantified under laboratory conditions. The coral fragments were first cultured in either low-nutrient (LN) or high-nutrient (HN) seawater condition at 27 degree C for 25days and the seawater temperature was then elevated and kept at 31 degree C for 1week for both nutrient conditions. Nutrient enrichment at 27 degree C increased the densities of symbiotic algae and chlorophyll a and the algal release rates (AR) from the host coral to the ambient seawater. The percentage of algal release rate to the standing stock (AR%) was approximately 0.015%h-1 for both nutrient conditions at 27 degree C. After the seawater temperature was elevated to 31 degree C, the densities of zooxanthellae in the corals decreased by 21-61%, and AR and AR% increased. The corals in HN lost more zooxanthellae per unit surface area than those in LN, but the lost percentages were not significantly different between LN and HN. AR% was also not significantly different between LN and HN at 31 degree C. These results indicated that the percentage rate of symbiotic algal loss was not affected by the nutrient condition, implying that nutrient incorporation itself would not accelerate coral bleaching susceptibility.
