TY  - JOUR
T1  - Excretion and respiration rates of the scyphomedusa Aurelia aurita from the Inland Sea of Japan
AN  - 19853999; 7335760
AB  - We measured the ammonium excretion, phosphate excretion and respiration rates of the scyphomedusa Aurelia aurita from Ondo Strait, in the central part of the Inland Sea of Japan, at 28 and 20 degree C. The rates measured at 28 degree C were converted to those at 20 degree C using the Q sub(10) values, i.e. 1.56, 1.57 and 2.80, for ammonium excretion, phosphate excretion and respiration rates, respectively. The composite relationships between metabolic rates and wet weight of a medusa (WW, g, range 11-1330 g) at 20 degree C were expressed by the following allometric equations. For ammonium excretion rate (N, mu moles N medusa super(-1)d super(-1)): N = 0.497WW super(1.09), phosphate excretion rate (P, mu moles P medusa super(-1)d super(-1)): P = 0.453WW super(0.84), and respiration rate (R, mu moles O sub(2) medusa super(-1)d super(-1)): R = 96.9WW super(1.06). Mean O:N ratios (i.e. atomic ratios of 16.9 and 11.0 at 28 and 20 degree C, respectively) indicated that the metabolism of A. aurita medusae was protein-dominated. These metabolic parameters enabled us to estimate the nitrogen and phosphorus regeneration rates of an A. aurita medusa population typical of early summer in the Ondo Strait (means of water temperature, medusa individual weight and population biomass: 20 degree C, 200 g WW and 50.8 g WW m super(-3), respectively). Regenerated nitrogen and phosphorus were equivalent to 10.0 and 21.6% of phytoplankton uptake rates, respectively, nearly twice that estimated for mesozooplankton, demonstrating that A. aurita medusae are key components of the plankton community, influencing the trophic and nutrient dynamics in the Ondo Strait during early summer.
