TY  - JOUR
T1  - Effects of grazing and excretion by pelagic mysids (Mysis spp.) on the size structure and biomass of the phytoplankton community
AN  - 17907311; 5865255
AB  - The aim of this study was to determine the effects of pelagic mysids (Mysis mixta and M. relicta) on the biomass and size-structure of the phytoplankton community during the period following the spring bloom. Mysids excreted phosphate (4.5 plus or minus 0.7 nmol ind super(-1) h super(-1)) and ammonium (123.6 plus or minus 31.6 and 45.0 plus or minus 3.2 nmol ind super(-1) h super(-1)) and increased the total chlorophyll-a concentration of phytoplankton slightly. However, the presence of mysids affected different size-classes of phytoplankton differently. Mysids mainly grazed on large-sized (>10 mu m) phytoplankton cells. Small-sized (<10 mu m) algal cells avoided grazing, gained a competitive advantage and were able to utilize the nutrients excreted by mysids. According to this study, both top-down and bottom-up mechanisms simultaneously mould the structure of the phytoplankton community. A large zooplankton biomass might promote the increase of small flagellates by a combination of repleting nutrient stores, selective grazing on large algal cells and heavy predation on protozoa which, consequently, might have a cascading effect on the most favoured protozoan food source, small flagellates.
