TY  - JOUR
T1  - Global chemical model analysis of biomass burning and lightning influences over the South Pacific in austral spring
AN  - 18623237; 5523724
AB  - A global three-dimensional model of tropospheric chemistry driven by reanalyzed European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts meteorological data is used to examine the sources of O sub(3), CO, and nitrogen oxides (NO sub(x) = NO + NO sub(2)) in the South Pacific troposphere during the NASA Pacific Exploratory Mission to the Tropics (PEM-Tropics A) in September-October 1996. Aircraft observations up to 12 km during that mission revealed considerable biomass burning influence on O sub(3) and CO in terms of elevated pollution layers and regional enhancements. The model reproduces the long-range transport of biomass burning effluents from southern Africa and South America in the westerly subtropical flow over the South Pacific. Meteorological conditions in 1996 were particularly favorable for this transport. Africa and South America make comparable contributions to the biomass burning pollution over the South Pacific; the contribution from Australia and Indonesia is much less. Biomass burning dominates the supply of NO sub(x) in the lower troposphere over the South Pacific (through long-range transport and decomposition of peroxyacetylnitrate), but lightning dominates in the upper troposphere. Observations in PEM-Tropics A and elsewhere indicate low HNO sub(3)/NO sub(x) concentration ratios and an imbalance in the chemical budget of NO sub(x) in the upper troposphere. We reproduce these observations in our model and show that they reflect the subsidence of primary NO sub(x) injected by lightning into the uppermost troposphere, rather than any fast chemistry recycling HNO sub(3) to NO sub(x). We find that biomass burning and lightning made similar contributions to O sub(3) production over the South Pacific during PEM-Tropics A. Biomass burning plumes sampled in PEM-Tropics A contained little NO sub(x), and the O sub(3) enhancements observed in these plumes originated from production over the source continents rather than over the South Pacific.
