TY  - JOUR
T1  - Effect of light and brine shrimp on skeletal delta super(13)C in the Hawaiian coral Porites compressa: a tank experiment
AN  - 17725282; 5492414
AB  - Previous experimental fieldwork showed that coral skeletal delta super(13)C values decreased when solar intensity was reduced, and increased in the absence of zooplankton. However, actual seasonal changes in solar irradiance levels are typically less pronounced than those used in the previous experiment and the effect of increases in the consumption of zooplankton in the coral diet on skeletal delta super(13)C remains relatively unknown. In the present study, the effects of four different light and heterotrophy regimes on coral skeletal delta super(13)C values were measured. Porites compressa corals were grown in outdoor flow-through tanks under 112%, 100%, 75%, and 50% light conditions at the Hawaii Institute of Marine Biology, Hawaii. In addition, corals were fed either zero, low, medium, or high concentrations of brine shrimp. Decreases in light from 100% resulted in significant decreases in delta super(13)C that is most likely due to a corresponding decrease in photosynthesis. Increases in light to 112% also resulted in a decrease in delta super(13)C values. This latter response may be a consequence of photoinhibition. The overall curved response in delta super(13)C values was described by a significant quadratic function. Increases in brine shrimp concentrations resulted in increased skeletal delta super(13)C levels. This unexpected outcome appears to be attributable to enhanced nitrogen supply associated with the brine shrimp diet which led to increased zooxanthellae concentrations, increased photosynthesis rates, and thus increased delta super(13)C values. This result highlights the potential influence of nutrients from heterotrophically acquired carbon in maintaining the zooxanthellae-host symbiosis in balance. In addition, evidence is presented that suggests that coral skeletal growth and delta super(13)C are decoupled. These results increase our knowledge of how light and heterotrophy affects the delta super(13)C of coral skeletons.
