TY  - JOUR
T1  - Ecological impact of green macroalgal blooms
AN  - 17343703; 4642093
AB  - Blooms of opportunistic macroalgae are an increasing feature of shallow-water marine areas. By virtue of their simple morphology and broad physiological tolerances, species within the genera Enteromorpha, Ulva, Chaetomorpha and Cladophora are able to utilize enhanced nutrients (nitrogen and phosphorus) and outcompete other seaweeds as well as seagrasses, and sometimes phytoplankton. High biomasses of bloom-forming macroalgae provide a refuge for small fishes, crustaceans and gastropods but generate a hostile physico-chemical environment in the underlying sediment. The associated sediment infauna responds dramatically: burrowing bivalves are forced to the surface and surface deposit feeders may be excluded. Blooms directly reduce the abundance of the invertebrate prey of fishes and shorebirds and also physically interfere with the feeding behaviour of some predators. Only a small proportion of the organic matter fixed by blooms is consumed by grazers and most of this material enters the local decomposer cycle or subsidizes more distant areas.
