TY  - JOUR
T1  - Hitch-hiking in the deep sea: A low energy-cost transportation system for some benthic foraminifers
AN  - 16540425; 4351045
AB  - Benthic foraminifers are heterotrophic, amoeboid protozoa living on the sea floor both of shallow and deep waters representing more than 50% of the eucaryotic biomass. Their diet includes a wide range of protozoans, crustaceans, fecal pellets, diatoms, radiolarians, tintinnids and even bacteria that colonize any sinking organic matter in the water column. The availability and quality of food particles especially for deep sea species without symbionts are limiting factors. In order to improve food particle access, some foraminifers developed interesting adaptations and behavioral strategies. Certain epifaunal species (e.g., Milionella subrotunda) use an elevated position through an agglutinated tube to utilize particles or remineralized substances horizontally transported by bottom currents. Rupertina stabilis enlarges and stabilizes its delicate pseudopodial net with sponge spicules to increase its catching potential and consequently its nutritional conditions. In this paper a new strategy for low energy-cost food gathering by three foraminifer species, Cibicidoides wuellersdorfi, Cibicides sp. and Discanomalina sp. is described.
