TY  - JOUR
T1  - Seasonal variation in the relative utilization of dietary protein and carbohydrate by the mussel, Mytilus edulis L
AN  - 16723819; 3712047
AB  - Mussels (Mytilus edulis) were collected from Whitsand Bay during April, July and September, 1993, and fed a defined diet of pre-killed microalgae and either [ super(15)N]protein/[ super(14)C]carbohydrate microcapsules (Diet A) or [ super(15)N][ super(14)C]protein/carbohydrate microcapsules (Diet B). Defecation, excretion, respiration and incorporation of both isotopes were measured to describe the mussel's relative utilization of dietary carbohydrate ( super(14)C in Diet A), protein-carbon ( super(14)C in Diet B) and protein-nitrogen ( super(15)N in Diets A and B). The assimilation efficiency for [ super(14)C]carbohydrate (16-18%) was less variable and significantly greater than the assimilation efficiency for [ super(14)C]protein (7-15%), and during September a greater proportion of assimilated super(14)C was respired from protein (29%) than from carbohydrate (17%). These results suggest that protein-carbon was not preferentially conserved from metabolism relative to dietary carbohydrates in these experiments, which were conducted during both spawning and post-spawning periods. In contrast, protein-nitrogen was always assimilated with greater efficiency (25-82%) than either [ super(14)C]protein or [ super(14)C]carbohydrate. The conservation of protein-nitrogen relative to protein-carbon was particularly noticeable in july when rates of ammonia excretion were lowest and [ super(15)N]protein assimilation was ten times more efficient than [ super(14)C]protein assimilation. Measured rates of whole-body protein synthesis were also greatest in July, which suggests that seasonal variation in protein-nitrogen conservation was related to the mussel's anabolic demand for nitrogen.
