TY  - JOUR
T1  - Regulation of nitrogen excretion of the amphibious Blenniidae Alticus kirki (Guenther, 1868) during emersion and immersion.
AN  - 16443927; 2908734
AB  - Of the five species of Blenniidae which are present in the intertidal zone of the Red Sea, the "leaping blenny" Alticus kirki meets the most extreme environmental conditions. It exhibits an amphibian lifestyle and during the day it emerges from the water to feed upon the algae growing in the splashzone on the rocks. Water contact exists only from waves splashing over the rocks. Nitrogen excretion of A. kirki , an amphibious blenny, was studied in relation to the duration of emersion. Nitrogen excretion of A. kirki was studied, during immersion, in relation to starvation. A. kirki excreted higher quantities of urea than fully aquatic fish both during immersion and during emersion. The ratio of ammonia to urea excretion changed with the duration of emersion. Nitrogen metabolism was reduced during emersion. This reduction was dependent on the emersion period.
