TY  - JOUR
T1  - DIN, DON and PO sub(4) flux by a medusa with algal symbionts.
AN  - 16512858; 2957537
AB  - Linuche unguiculata , a coronate scyphozoan with symbiotic dinoflagellates (= zooxanthellae), was shown to be capable of taking up NH sub(4), NO sub(3), PO sub(4) and 3 amino acids from seawater. The algae constitute 32% of the total organismal nitrogen. Both depletion and uptake experiments showed the flux of NH sub(4) to be more than that of NO sub(3). Rates of super(15)NH sub(4) uptake by freshly isolated zooxanthellae were comparable to those for free-living phytoplankton, but super(15)NO sub(3) uptake was much lower. Medusae held in the dark several days demonstrated a substantial decrease in their uptake (or depletion) of NH sub(4) and PO sub(4). The uptake of super(15)N-labelled glycine and leucine and super(14)C-labelled alanine was measured at a range of concentrations at rates similar to those measured for NH sub(4). Fractionation procedures after uptake experiments showed that both non-algal and zooxanthellae fractions were labelled within a few minutes but super(15)N from NH sub(4) appeared preferentially in the zooxanthellar fraction whereas nitrogen from amino acids went primarily to the non-algal (supernatant) fraction.
