TY  - JOUR
T1  - Self- and mutual shading effect on competing algal distribution.
AN  - 16040929; 2614817
AB  - Shading of light by algae growing in a watercolumn plays an interesting and important role in the dynamics of algal blooms. In natural waters many species of algae compete not only for light but also for nutrients. The presence of a species in water can attenuate light intensity not only on itself through self- and mutual shading effect, but also for other species that are present. The authors model algal populations which compete for nutrients and prove the existence of a unique positive global mild solution using techniques of semigroup theory. They prove the existence of nonnegative steady-state solutions to the model system and identify the criteria for existence of the solutions.
