TY  - JOUR
T1  - The physiological and bioenergetic responses of blue crab larvae to diet and seasonality.
AN  - 15703602; 2333022
AB  - Larvae hatched in different seasons were reared on two diets. Both consisted of algal-fed rotifers and brine shrimp nauplii, but one was supplemented with a long chain, w3 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) product. Food items were analyzed in terms of energy, carbon and nitrogen content. For each diet, respiration, ammonia excretion, feeding and growth rates were measured in each larval stage. These and other parameters were then compared between diets in order to examine the effects of food quality on physiology and bioenergetics. Preliminary data indicate that the two diets differ very little in energy content. This suggests that the long chain, w3 PUFA do not directly supply energy, but affect the efficiency of food utilization. This reduces respiration rates and the amount of energy required for basal metabolism, which in turn allows more energy to be used for growth and development. This effect could, therefore, influence larval survival and the magnitude of juvenile recruitment.
