TY  - JOUR
T1  - Nitrogen excretion and O:N ratios in reef corals: Evidence for conservation of nitrogen.
AN  - 15616365; 2260182
AB  - Rates of ammonia excretion, and respiration to excretion (atomic O:N) ratios were measured for three species of scleractinian coral from the Bahamas, during Aug. 1986 and Mar. 1987. Freshly collected individuals of 2 reef spp., Montastrea annularis and Acropora cervicornis , have significantly lower mean ammonia excretion rates (51 plus or minus 66 nmol/(mg-at N)/h and 192 plus or minus 172 nmol/(mg-at N)/h, resp.) than those of the tropical nonzooxanthellate spp. Tubastrea coccinea (257 plus or minus 68 nmol/(mg-at N)/h). The temperate nonzooxanthellate coral Astrangia poculata has mean excretion rates (632 plus or minus 242 nmol/(mg-at N)/h) which are much higher than those of all three tropical species. O:N ratios for the 2 reef spp. were generally > 300, while those of the nonzooxanthellate species ranged from 17 to 39 for T. coccinea and from 8 to 12 for A. poculata .
