TY  - JOUR
T1  - Increasing growth rates and survival of giant clams in mariculture.
AN  - 15293995; 2053192
AB  - Giant clams of the family Tridacnidae are unique among animal species in mariculture in that they have an internal food source in the form of symbiotic algae (zooxanthellae of the genus Symbiodinium ) that provide a significant portion of their nutrition. Recent efforts to improve growth and survival rates of tridacnids have centered on selection of species and strains of Symbiodinium and additions of inorganic nutrients. Results show that some strains of Symbiodinium living in symbiosis with Tridacna gigas and Hippopus hippopus yield growth and survival rates of clams 3-5 times those of symbioses with other strains. When inorganic nitrogen in the form of ammonia is added, growth rates of clams almost double compared to controls.
