TY  - JOUR
T1  - Effects of deep sea water on the growth of a marine diatom species Skeletonema costatum .
AN  - 15068602; 1867999
AB  - Batch culture experiments were carried out using a marine diatom species, Skeletonema costatum , in filtered seawater samples collected from various depths of 0.5, 20, 40, 60, 80, 150, 200, 300, 400, 500 and 600 m. S. costatum grew to reach a final cell concentration of 35.6 x 10 super(4) cells/ml with a specific growth rate ( mu ) of 0.72/day in the filtered seawater collected from 600 m, although the culture exhibited a lag period in the growth. Enrichment experiments using the filtered 600 m seawater revealed that none of the trace metals, vitamins and TRIS gave any substantial effect on the growth of S. costatum) but EDTA over 100 mu g/1, an optimum being 200 mu g/1, significantly reduced the duration of lag period. The results suggest that deep sea water is deficient in organic chelator which reduces the lag period, and that S. costatum cells excrete some organic chelator to make the deep sea water more suitable to growth. Specific growth rate and final cell yield of S. costatum were both depressed at the shallower depths due to a deficiency of macronutrients.
