TY  - JOUR
T1  - Mechanisms of photoadaptation in three strains of the symbiotic dinoflagellate Symbiodinium microadriaticum .
AN  - 14041591; 875457
AB  - Mechanisms of photoadaptation of photosynthesis have been studied in 3 strains of the symbiotic dinoflagellate S. microadriaticum . Algal strains were isolated from the clam Tridacna maxima , the sea anemone Aiptasia pulchella , and the scleractinian coral Montipora verrucosa were maintained in the defined medium ASP-8A and were grown at irradiances ranging from 22 to 248 mu E m super(-2)/s super(-1) on a 14 h: 10 h (light:dark) photoperiod at 26 degree C. All algal cultures were analyzed during log-phase of growth. At all light levels, rates of cell division and photosynthesis were determined, as were cell volumes, pigmentation, and carbonated nitrogen content. Low light-induced changes in pigmentation were evident to varying degrees in all three algal strains, although alterations in the photosynthesis-irradiance relations were distinctly different in each strain. The algae from T. maxima show the least photoadaptive capability, and seem to photoadapt by changing photosynthetic unit (PSU) size. The adaptive capabilities of the algae appear to correlate well with the ecological distribution of their respective hosts.
