TY  - JOUR
T1  - Uric acid utilization in Platymonas convolutae and symbiotic Convoluta roscoffensis .
AN  - 13698309; 570833
AB  - Uric acid is an excellent nitrogen source for the growth of cultures of Platymonas convolutae , the symbiotic alga from Convoluta roscoffensis , and related Platymonas and Tetraselmis species. Nitrate-grown cells of P. convolutae and T. tetrathele have two uptake systems for uric acid. In darkness, intact cells of P. convolutae metabolize (2- super(14)C)uric acid to ( super(14)C)carbon dioxide. These results are consistent with the proposal that the algal symbionts of C. roscoffensis utilize uric acid, received from the host, as a nitrogen source. Aposymbiotic juvenile and symbiotic adult C. roscoffensis under standard culture conditions contain uric acid. The solid uric acid content of juveniles declines on establishment of symbiosis with P. convolutae and the endogenous uric acid is utilized in the adult symbiosis under conditions of nitrogen demand. It is proposed that uric acid utilization is of significance to the growth of the developing symbiosis in a nitrogen-poor environment.
