TY  - JOUR
T1  - Effects of Copper and Zinc on Growth, Morphology, and Metabolism of Asterionella japonica (Cleve)
AN  - 15245690; 52657
AB  - When exposed to elevated levels of copper or zinc, the diatom Asterionella japonica showed a reduced cell division rate and a marked increase in cell size. Metal-treated cells had greater cell volumes, dry weights, carbon, nitrogen, chlorophyll, and DNA contents, all in approximately the same proportion as control cells. Metal-treated cells showed an uncoupling of photosynthesis from cell division and continued to enlarge when fixed carbon could not be excreted or utilized in cell division. Uptake of sulphate and silicic acid proceeded at slower rates than other processes in copper-treated cells. Copper-treated cells resuspended in fresh medium shrank to normal size when exposed to methionine (which they accumulated), although cell division rates did not return to normal. Cells treated with the sulfhydryl binder PCMB divided at reduced rates and also swelled in manner comparable to copper-treated cells.
