TY  - JOUR
T1  - Nutrients in seagrass beds in Miskito Cays, Nicaragua.
AN  - 14670825; 1541730
AB  - The plant community in the 2 stations adjacent to the mangroves was dominated by monospecific stands of Thalassia testudinum with high biomass whereas the shelf stations contained mixed stands of T. testudinum and Syringodium filiforme with low biomass. Plant detritus in this transect varies from 241 g dry/m super(2) in the innermost station to 11 g dry/m super(2) in the outer station. Interstitial sediment nutrients and related chemical parameters paralleled this gradient. The inner, lagoon stations were the most reducing habitats and contained the highest concentration of nitrogen, primarily as ammonium, and hydrogen sulfide and had the lowest pH values. It is concluded that the plant detritus gradient, due to the subsidy from mangroves, was responsible for the observed changes in sediment chemistry and subsequently the plant community.
AU  - McRoy, C P
AD  - Inst. Mar. Sci., Univ. Alaska, Fairbanks, AK 99701, USA
