TY  - JOUR
T1  - Primary production off the coast of northwest Africa: excretion of dissolved organic matter and its heterotrophic uptake
AN  - 15035029; 06284344
AB  - The flux of carbon in a eutrophic coastal upwelling region was studied in the Cap Blanc area of northwest Africa from March through May 1974. As part of the primary productivity measurements using radioactive carbon, the release of dissolved organic carbon was measured at 38 stations with 6- and 24-h incubations. The average excretion for the 24-h incubations was 8 . 16 mg C m SUP--2 h SUP--1 , 8 . 7% of the total carbon fixed. Excretion covaried with carbon fixation but was not directly influenced by varying nutrient or light conditions. Heterotrophic uptake was computed by means of a carbon mass flux model based on the difference between the rates of accumulation of excretion products in the light and in the dark. This model indicates that bacteria removed 17 . 8% of the total amount of excreted matter per hour, representing an uptake of 1 . 45 mg C m SUP--2 h SUP--1 . This is 1 . 7% of the autotrophic particulate production rate. The total bacterioplankton production available for grazing by the food chain will be <1 . 7% of the autotrophic production, because bacterial respiration and cell maintenance will consume part of the organic uptake. The data indicate that bacterioplankton production has a minor role in the particulate carbon budget of upwelling regions. Conversely, the carbon mass flux model indicates that the heterotrophic bacterial uptake of phytoplankton excretion products regulates the concentration of dissolved organic carbon in the euphotic zone and prevents the accumulation of dissolved material during phytoplankton blooms. In this upwelling region heterotrophs play a major role in determining the chemical character of the water, but they play a minor role in the flux of carbon through the food web.
JF  - Deep-Sea Res., 24(1), 35-47, (1977)
