TY  - JOUR
T1  - Azolla-Anabaena azollae relationship.
AN  - 18001575; 5263914
AB  - The water fern, Azolla caroliniana Willd., containing the symbiotic, heterocystous blue-green alga, Anabaena azollae, has been studied under various growth conditions to characterize its light-dependent production of H Sub(2). The response of H Sub(2) production to N Sub(2) and C Sub(2)H Sub(2) and the absence of a differential effect of m-chlorocarbonyl cyanide phenylhydrazone on H Sub(2) production and C Sub(2)H Sub(2) reduction, coupled with the parallel inhibition of both processes by DCMU imply that the production of H Sub(2) is nitrogenase-catalyzed and ATP-dependent. H Sub(2) was produced by fronds grown under air-CO Sub(2) in the presence of absence of combined nitrogen. When cultured under argon-O Sub(2)-CO Sub(2), only those fronds provided with combined nitrogen remained viable and produced H Sub(2). Fronds grown on nitrate under air plus 2% CO also produced H Sub(2). In comparison to fronds grown on N Sub(2) alone, fronds grown on nitrate had an increased rate of H Sub(2) production relative to C Sub(2)H Sub(2) reduction, and the inhibition of H Sub(2) production by air was less. CO in argon +- CO Sub(2) resulted in a partial inhibition of H Sub(2) production, whereas CO in argon-CO Sub(2)-C Sub(2)H Sub(2) enhanced H Sub(2) production in fronds grown without combined nitrogen. The authors' studies strongly indicate that H Sub(2) production is nitrogenase-catalyzed but the possibility that the symbiont contains a hydrogenase cannot be totally excluded.
