TY  - RPRT
T1  - Phytoplankton-zooplankton relationships in 100-liter plastic bags
AN  - 15043767; 06291229
AB  - The functioning of plankton systems enclosed in 100 l plastic bags was investigated during spring, summer, and fall in an embayment on the Swedish east coast. Data from experimental systems, in which the normal phytoplankton: zooplankton ratios were changed, were compared with data from control bags to examine relationships among primary production, phytoplankton growth, and changes in organic carbon and phosphorus. In bags which were enriched with zooplankton, there was evidence of higher grazing pressures as well as positive effects on primary productivity. SUP-14 C uptake in the control bags varied from 25 to 75 mgC/m SUP-3 day and was generally higher by as much as 10-fold in the bag enriched with zooplankton. Stimulatory effects of higher zooplankton concentrations were indicated by higher productivity/chlorophyll ratios and, in some cases, faster rates of phosphate cycling. SUP-14 C productivity accounted for about 60% of the total gross production indicating that a substantial amount was respired by the phytoplankton and/or excreted as dissolved organic carbon. Direct evidence of grazing pressure was found in phytoplankton growth rates. In bags enriched with zooplankton there were lower growth rates with a minimum of minus 20%/day due to the partial disappearance of blue-green algae. In bags with dilute zooplankton stocks there was generally a rapid increase of phytoplankton biomass (up to +170%/day) due to the growth of monads, Peridinium and Dinobryon. A significant increase of monads was characteristic in both control and experimental bags. These data are discussed with special reference to a summary model of conditions in the control bags. Phyto-zooplankton relationships are discussed as a reward-loop interaction among productivity, grazing, and nutrient recycle.
JF  - Publ.by: Univ. Stockholm, Stockholm (Sweden), 1976, 83 p., Contrib. Asko Lab., Univ. Stockholm
