TY  - JOUR
T1  - Comparative evaluation of certain organic and inorganic sources of nitrogen for phototrophic growth of marine microalgae.
AN  - 17945295; 5231249
AB  - 26 spp of marine planktonic algae, belonging to 8 taxonomic divisions, were tested under axenic-culture conditions for their capacity to grow, under cool white light, on nitrate, nitrite, ammonium, urea, glycine, D- glucosamine or hypoxanthine as sloe N Sub(2) source. The culture medium was composed of Mediterranean oligotrophic sea water enriched with phosphate, silicate, trace-metal ions and vitamins, and buffered at pH 7.5-8.0. Each N compd was tested at a fixed conc of 500 {mu}g-at, N/litre. All the spp showed good growth on ammonium, and 25 spp showed equally good growth on nitrate and nitrite; Hemiselmis virescens was exceptional in being unable to use nitrate and nitrite. Of the spp tested, 88% showed generally good growth on urea. often better than on ammonium; the exceptions, unable to use urea, where Chlomydomonas palla, Cyclotella cryptica and a strain of Porphyridium. 69% of the spp indicated growth-supporting potential of hypoxanthine, generally comparable to that of ammonium; many of these spp required long or significant periods of adaptation to the purine before commencing growth, while others sharted growth as soon as for nitrate or ammonium. 50% of the spp showed light to heavy growth on glycine, and from these only 3 spp appeared to require significant adaptation. None of the spp showed good growth on glucosamine despite sufficient time for adaptation; 42% of the app showed light growth equivalent to about 15-35% of the growth obtained on ammonium, The physiological and ecological significance of the results is discussed. it is suggested that the excretion of ammonia and urea by marine fauna and their utilization by phytoplankton may be ecologically important, and the same may be true, to a lesser extent, of hyposanthine and glycine.
