TY  - JOUR
T1  - The growth of Skeletonema costatum during a winter-spring bloom in Narragansett Bay, Rhode Island.
AN  - 17778628; 4832207
AB  - Phytoplankton, zooplankton, and 4 nutrients were analyzed weekly; 14 experiments using wild populations were incubated in situ. Rates of diatom growth, predation, and NH sub(3)-N excretion by the copepod Acartia clausi, and the influence of temperature, light, and nutrients on the growth of Skeletonema costatum were determined. Skeletonema's growth calculated from nutrient uptake kinetics was compared to observed rates. Diatom predation by an ebridian is reported. In vitro growth studies with S. costatum, grazing by A. clausi, and preliminary coaction studies using Phaeocystis poucheti and three diatoms are also presented. Temp, light, nutrients, grazing, possibly species interactions, and hydrographic disturbances regulated the seasonal dynamics of S. costatum. N and Si limited the growth of S. costatum from mid-March through mid-June. A. clausi excreted NH sub(3)-N at rates inversely related to temp and diatom abundance. From 2.6 to 78.6 per cent of the daily N required for S. costatum's growth was excreted by zooplankton.
