TY  - JOUR
T1  - A study of plankton dynamics and nutrient cycling in the central gyre of the North Pacific Ocean.
AN  - 17777428; 4833055
AB  - The dynamics of phytoplankton growth in relation to nutrient concs were studied in the subtropical central gyre of the North Pacific in Nov 1971. Rates of excretion of phosphate, ammonium, and urea-N by zooplankton and rates of assimilation of carbon, nitrate, ammonium, and urea-N by phytoplankton were measured. The growth rate of phytoplankton was estimated to be approx. 0.2-0.3 doublings day super(1) in the 70-80-m mixed layer, apparently limited by concs of both N and phosphate. Only N conc was so limiting at a station near the western edge of the California Current. No diel changes in concs of ambient nutrients were observed. Urea-N appears to be an important source of N for phytoplankton growth in these waters and to be an important excretory product of zooplankton. Concs of phosphate and ammonium were extremely low, but turnover times were estimated to be approx. 3-5 days for ammonium and greater than 10 days for urea and phosphate. Biomass of phytoplankton in the mixed layer was also very low, and corresponded approximately to that expected if a lab culture were operated as a N-limited chemostat with a conc of approx. 0.48 mu g-atom N liter super(-l) in the incoming culture medium and a dilution rate of approx. 0.13 per day. Physiological differences were noted between the phytoplankton in the mixed layer and that living below the thermocline, as were differences in chemical composition (ratio of C:Chl a and C:N).
