TY  - JOUR
T1  - The nutrient cycle in coastal water.
AN  - 17789549; 4837725
AB  - Although the area of coastal water and offshore water of high productivity covers only 10 per cent of the world ocean, half of the world's fish catch is made from these waters, while the other half is from upwelling regions as calculated by Ryther (1969). Even though his calculation involved some uncertainties, his data clearly show how important the coastal water is in the world ocean. Naturally, it is of special significance that many investigations have been made of the coastal water and particularly the efforts are extended for furthering the knowledge of nutrient cycle in relation to the production of organic matter there. The source of nutrient needed for the primary production in the euphotic zone is partly in bacterial decomposition of organic matter and excretion of zooplankton occurring in the zone itself and partly in the supply from deeper layers by vertical mixing and from atmospheric precipitation. Among these sources, the supply from bacterial decomposition and zooplankton excretion is generally accepted play a dominant role excepting such areas where divergence or other hydrographic features to bring nutrient rich subsurface waters into the euphotic zone exist. In the case of coastal water, some additional points must be taken into account. One is the inflow of river water which bring a considerable amount of major elements with some minor metals and dissolved organic matters into the coastal water. The added chemical substances contribute greatly to sustaining a high fertility and large population of primary producers. The variation of rainfall in the drainage area often causes changes of phytoplankton communities quantitatively and qualitatively. For instance, the occurrence of red tide is often observed following a heavy rainfall. Increased pollution of river water also adds new factors to disrupt the existing nutrient system in coastal water. Sea bottom constitutes another point when the cycle of nutrient in coastal water is considered. Nutrients are supplied by the activity of microorganisms in the bottom mud and benthic animals, while part of nutrients is used by seaweeds and other benthic algae growing at the bottom. The artificial culture of laver, oyster, pearl and others also greatly affects the nutrient cycle in surroundings through the activity of uptake and release of nutrient.
