TY  - JOUR
T1  - On the nutrition and metabolism of zooplankton. 7. Seasonal survey of nitrogen and phosphorus excretion by Calanus in the Clyde sea area.
AN  - 1521389698; 5522394
AB  - Females, males and stage V Calanus finmarchicus (Gunnerus) and C. helgolandicus (Claus) have been collected near Garroch Head in the Clyde sea-area at all seasons, and measurements made of body weight, nitrogen and phosphorus content and levels of nitrogen and phosphorus excretion. Evidence that high food levels induce greater excretion rates was obtained from field results and from laboratory experiments with freshly caught and starved animals. During winter, when the level of plant food was very low in the sea, starved and freshly caught animals excreted both nitrogen and phosphorus at roughly the same low rates: but in spring, when plant food was plentiful, freshly caught animals excreted nitrogen and phosphorus at rates much higher than starved. The proportion of 'organic' phosphorus excreted increased considerably in spring (1969) when the conc of plant food rose above a certain level (approx 10 super(6) cells/l), and reached a maximum of over 70 percent of the total phosphorus excreted. No concomitant rise in the proportion of organic nitrogen excreted was observed, 88 percent of the total still being ammonia. The extra amounts of phosphorus excreted during the spring diatom bloom were nearly all 'organic' phosphorus: the levels of inorganic phosphorus excreted in the spring were much the same as those found for excreted total phosphorus in winter. The ratio N:P in soluble excretion products did not significantly differ for females (5.14:1), males (5.34:1) and V's (4.68:1). However, the average spring value for the 3 stages combined (4.96:1)was significantly lower than that found in winter (6.60:1). Additional quantities of nitrogen and phosphorus were laid down by all three stages during the spring diatom peak in the ratio 12.5:1; the ratio in the faecal pellets produced by these animals was 13.2:1, and that of the spring phytoplankton 8.05:1. These values have been used to show that 77 percent of the phosphorus and 62.4 percent of the nitrogen captured by the animals during the spring was assimilated. Of the total quantity of nitrogen captured each day by Calanus in late April, 35.7 percent was excreted in soluble form, 37.5 percent was lost as faecal pellets and 26.8 percent was invested in growth. Corresponding values in terms of phosphorus were 59.8 percent, 23.0 percent and 17.2 percent respectively. Female and stage V Calanus require 8.4 percent body nitrogen and 13.6 percent body phosphorus daily in spring to compensate for metabolic losses and ensure growth. The corresponding values in winter are only 1.93-2.80 percent (N) and 7.23-6.20 percent (P). The assimilation efficiencies of the animals are such as to allow them to obtain these daily quantities by sweeping clear 29.0 ml sea water/day in spring and 37-58 ml/day in winter. Some feeding must take place in winter, although plant food is virtually absent.
